  "body_mappings": [
    {
      "section": "en",
      "page": 2,
      "bbox": [
        37.0,
        518.4614173228347,
        461.8976377952756,
        597.6614173228347
      ],
      "text": "In 1941 George de Mestral, a Swiss engineer, returned from hunting in the Alps with his hound, an Irish Pointer. Prickly burdock seed-heads had hitched themselves to his clothes and lay tangled in the dog's fur. Under a microscope he saw thousands of tiny barbs, each a hook grasping the small loops in cloth."
    },
    {
      "section": "en",
      "page": 2,
      "bbox": [
        37.0,
        430.7614173228347,
        461.8976377952756,
        509.9614173228347
      ],
      "text": "Few took his idea seriously at first. In Lyon, then a weaving centre, a cotton version proved too frail to survive abrasion. Working with a textiles firm, he turned to nylon, whose hooks were stiff enough to hold yet easy to pull apart. The trial-and-error at the looms and mills lasted about ten years."
    },
    {
      "section": "en",
      "page": 2,
      "bbox": [
        37.0,
        343.0614173228347,
        461.8976377952756,
        422.2614173228347
      ],
      "text": "The name Velcro merged two French words, velours, meaning velvet, and crochet, meaning hook. A US patent duly followed in 1955. In 1959 a New York fashion show sent apparel stitched with the fasteners down the runway. The public was less charmed than expected, mostly because of the aesthetic."
    },
    {
      "section": "en",
      "page": 2,
      "bbox": [
        37.0,
        255.3614173228347,
        461.8976377952756,
        334.5614173228347
      ],
      "text": "The underdog proved its utility in outer space. NASA used it in the Apollo programme to keep gear anchored in weightlessness, so that nothing would hover in mid-air. Apollo 11 was equipped with a sizeable amount, about 3,300 square inches. NASA did not invent the nylon clasp."
    },
    {
      "section": "en",
      "page": 2,
      "bbox": [
        37.0,
        147.8614173228347,
        461.8976377952756,
        246.8614173228347
      ],
      "text": "When the patent ran out in 1978, cut-price knock-offs from other countries overran the market, and a niche product went mainstream. In 2017, to stop its brand-name melting into a generic noun, the company asked people to call such mimics hook and loop fasteners. In effect, the firm was asking the public to stop using its own name for rivals' goods."
    },
    {
      "section": "zh",
      "page": 3,
      "bbox": [
        37.0,
        517.6614173228347,
        461.8976377952756,
        597.6614173228347
      ],
      "text": "1941年，乔治·德·梅斯特拉尔，一位瑞士的工程师，带着他的猎犬，一只爱尔兰的指示犬，从阿尔卑斯山的打猎中归来。多刺的牛蒡种子头已把它们自己钩在了他的衣服上，并缠绕在狗的毛皮里。在显微镜下，他看到成千上万个细小的倒刺，每一个（如同）一个钩子，抓住布料上的小圈。"
    },
    {
      "section": "zh",
      "page": 3,
      "bbox": [
        37.0,
        429.1614173228347,
        461.8976377952756,
        509.1614173228347
      ],
      "text": "起初，很少有人认真看待他的想法。在里昂，当时的一个纺织中心，一个棉制版本被证明太脆弱，以至于无法在磨损中存活下来。与一家纺织品公司合作，他转向了尼龙，其钩子足够坚硬且不易弯曲，既能扣住，又容易拉开。在织机旁和工厂里的反复试验持续了约十年。"
    },
    {
      "section": "zh",
      "page": 3,
      "bbox": [
        37.0,
        360.6614173228347,
        461.8976377952756,
        420.6614173228347
      ],
      "text": "Velcro 这个名字合并了两个法语词，velours 意为天鹅绒，crochet 意为钩子。1955年，一项美国专利如期而至。1959年，纽约一场时装秀让缝有这种搭扣的服装走上T台。公众却不如预期那样着迷，主要是因为它的审美。"
    },
    {
      "section": "zh",
      "page": 3,
      "bbox": [
        37.0,
        272.1614173228347,
        461.8976377952756,
        352.1614173228347
      ],
      "text": "这个不被看好的（发明）在外太空证明了自己的用处。美国航天局在阿波罗计划中使用了它，以便在失重状态下保持装备固定，从而没有任何东西会在半空中飘浮。阿波罗11号配备了相当多的用量，约3,300平方英寸。美国航天局并没有发明这种尼龙扣件。"
    },
    {
      "section": "zh",
      "page": 3,
      "bbox": [
        37.0,
        183.66141732283472,
        461.8976377952756,
        263.6614173228347
      ],
      "text": "1978年专利到期后，来自其他国家的廉价的仿制品/山寨品泛滥于市场，一种小众产品成了主流。2017年，为了阻止其品牌名逐渐消融为一个通用的名词，该公司要求人们把这类仿品称作为“钩毛搭扣”。实际上，这家公司是在请求公众停止将其自己的名字用在对手的商品上。"
    }
  ],
